---
// The story: why git.wtf counts fuck-ups now. On the homepage it has its heading and ends with the way into the
// old blog; at the top of the archive, which is the old blog, it sits under the page's title and ends on the
// archive's own line instead.
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { href } from '../lib/site';
interface Props { toArchive?: boolean; head?: boolean }
const { toArchive = true, head = true } = Astro.props;
const posts = (await getCollection('archive')).filter((p) => p.data.type === 'post').length;
---
<div class="col">
    {head && <div class="beat-head"><h2 id="story-h" data-lock>The story</h2></div>}
    <div class={`story${head ? '' : ' story-top'}`}>
        <p>For 10 years git.wtf was a blog about fixing Git, written for people who had <em>just</em> fucked up and were looking for the fix. Then AI came into the picture and started doing the fixes for them, and the questions stopped coming here.</p>
        <p><mark>We noticed, envied and wanted revenge.</mark> So we started counting AI's fuck-ups alongside the Git fuck-ups.</p>
        <p>AI now runs Git commands by itself, and <mark>a good many of the fuck-ups are now its own</mark>. We keep track of those with delight.</p>
        {toArchive ? <p>By the way, the old blog posts are still here, unchanged, in the archive.</p> : <p>These are those posts, unchanged and at their old addresses. The commands still work. The jokes are older.</p>}
    </div>
    {toArchive && <p class="story-go"><a class="story-btn" href={href('/archive/')}>Read the old blog: {posts} posts</a></p>}
</div>
